WEBVTT

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:27.790
Today's lead is Double Blind Evals, a pilot that lets an evaluator test a proprietary model on a secret benchmark without either side seeing the other's asset.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:43.415
The two next most consequential papers are Beyond A U R O C, which shows that headline detection scores for jailbreak detectors say little about deployment, and a paper on mitigating agentic deception by training an adaptive monitor against an agent.

00:00:43.415 --> 00:00:50.790
Ten papers in all, including a note on look ahead in online algorithms that has almost nothing to do with governance.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:17.310
The lead paper is Double Blind Evals: Resolving the Dual Confidentiality Dilemma in AI Safety Auditing. It is a short systems and pilot report from authors at the A I Verification and Evaluation Research Institute, Google, Singapore's A I Safety Institute, OpenMined and M L Commons. Category: A I governance.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:35.260
The problem is a standing bind in third party auditing. An evaluator wants its benchmark prompts to stay secret, because leaked prompts end up in training data and inflate scores. A developer will not hand over weights, because they are expensive, proprietary and potentially dual use.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:51.010
Call the developer's API and the developer sees every prompt in plain text. Ask for the weights and you will almost certainly be refused. The authors note that, to their knowledge, no external evaluator has ever received frontier weights in the clear to protect a benchmark.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:02:05.485
They motivate the secrecy with two kinds of contamination. On deliberate gaming, they cite work finding that one frontier lab tested twenty seven private model variants on Chatbot Arena and published only the top scorer.

00:02:05.485 --> 00:02:24.610
On accidental leakage, they cite a study finding strong evidence of benchmark leakage in pre training or post training for roughly half of thirty one models tested, and a later study showing that contamination inflates measured performance, with the inflation growing with both the amount of contamination and the model size.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:47.785
The mechanism is a hardware enclave. The setup runs on Google Cloud Confidential Space, on an A three high G P U instance with Intel T D X encrypting host memory and an NVIDIA H one hundred confidential G P U, orchestrated with OpenMined's PySyft. The chip has secrets burned in at manufacture, from which it derives a key the manufacturer certifies.

00:02:47.785 --> 00:02:59.435
Each boot, it generates a fresh memory encryption key that is destroyed at shutdown. It also signs a hash of the software it is running, so outside parties can see what will handle their data.

00:03:00.085 --> 00:03:15.410
The authors are clear that an enclave alone does not give you double blindness. The signed hash only means something if the software inside cannot leak. So the whole stack, from firmware to the application container, has to be trusted, and they build that trust with three things.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:29.835
First, measurement: each layer is hashed as it loads, and the hashes go into an attestation report signed by a key rooted in the chip vendor, with an ephemeral public key embedded so the user's secure channel is tied to the attested software.

00:03:29.835 --> 00:03:46.010
Second, reproducible builds, so any third party can rebuild each layer and check it gets the same measurement. Third, verification: each user independently checks the signature chain, a freshness nonce and the measurements, rather than trusting anyone else's verdict.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:04:01.085
The lowest layers, processor microcode and secure processor firmware, are closed and cannot be rebuilt, so the chip vendor stays in the trust root. The authors argue that is unavoidable, and constrained, because that layer is identical for every user.

00:04:01.735 --> 00:04:15.835
The harder step is that in an evaluation, each party contributes code, not just data. The model owner brings inference code and the evaluator brings eval code, and both may be trade secrets. The bridge is an allowlist argument.

00:04:15.835 --> 00:04:35.460
If hidden code can only call methods from a list of non network, non leaking operations, such as tensor operations in PyTorch or Jax, then it cannot send data out of the enclave even though its contents stay secret. A library called syft restrict verifies the redaction, and PySyft handles submission and approval.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:05:01.285
The procedure has seven steps: both parties gather their assets, one party launches the enclave and gets only the power to switch it off, each side checks the signed software hash, optionally each uploads a mock of its model or benchmark to develop against, each uploads possibly redacted code and private assets, each approves the other's redacted code, and only when both have approved does the enclave run and release results to whichever party was chosen.

00:05:01.935 --> 00:05:13.410
Now the pilot. The model was Google DeepMind's Gemini two point five Flash Lite, served by Google's model server inside the enclave, with weights streamed in at initialisation.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:28.485
The benchmark came from the reserve set of M L Commons' A I Luminate, prompts that have never been processed by any model, covering chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear and explosive hazards, cyberattacks, hate speech, self harm and violent crime.

00:05:28.485 --> 00:05:43.810
The Institute encrypted and decrypted the prompts and outputs and its staff evaluated the outputs. A second run used a private prompt set on harmful content elicitation in Singapore's context, with Singapore's A I Safety Institute.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:06:06.710
The authors call this the first use of the technology to give mutual secrecy between one organisation's frontier class proprietary weights and another's closed benchmark. They build on a 2024 OpenMined pilot with the U K A I Safety Institute and Anthropic, which evaluated G P T two on a five row sample and left the two sided protection problem open.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:23.310
What the paper does not contain matters as much. It reports no benchmark scores, no latency or cost measured by the authors, and no formal security proof. The under five percent compute overhead is a figure cited from NVIDIA. The authors list their own gaps.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:38.785
Running Gemini using only open source layers was judged too large an engineering task, so some proprietary method implementations remained and not all code could be inspected or allowlisted; the Institute was told and accepted the setup.

00:06:38.785 --> 00:06:52.160
The Confidential Space guest operating system has published reference values and an externally validated build pipeline, but individual builds are not independently reproducible, because they take private signing keys.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:07:11.535
And the attestation report is signed and verified through Google's services, which puts Google, who is also the model owner here and the host, in the verification path. There is also an internal inconsistency: the text names the benchmark version as one point zero in the introduction and one point four in the setup section.

00:07:12.185 --> 00:07:27.760
On where this goes, the authors say the bottleneck is no longer compute but procedure: legal agreements and cross organisation code review. They want standardised attestation pipelines with near zero human overhead, an analogue of the browser lock icon.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:46.410
Scaling to models beyond a trillion parameters means distributed confidential clusters of many H one hundred or B two hundred nodes. And, speculatively, they suggest that if a model under test tried to alter its harness, weights or sandbox, the enclave hash would no longer match and the instance would halt.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:08:07.610
The governance read. This is the most directly usable paper of the day. It is a concrete mechanism for what audit regimes keep asking for, access that does not depend on trusting either side's contracts or logging promises, and it is the sort of infrastructure national A I safety institutes could require. But hold the claim at its actual strength.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:27.060
One small model, no results, a trust root that includes Google and the chip vendors, and a pilot that did not meet its own code inspection standard. My inference: the interesting policy question is who runs the reproducible builds and the attestation verification, because that party becomes the real auditor of the audit.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:36.760
And a model small enough to be run this way says little yet about frontier scale, where memory, multi node interconnect and architecture secrecy all get harder.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:52.230
Next, Can A I be a Researcher Without Borders? Auditing Bias in A I Assisted Social Science Research about China and the U S. Category: A I governance.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:09:10.830
Five consumer tier language models, two American, two Chinese and one European, wrote two hundred academic style essays on ten social science questions under four prompt framings. Two expert annotators coded them for national favouritism and classified the sources each answer used.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:21.905
The models were an OpenAI G P T model, a Google Gemini model, DeepSeek, Alibaba's Qwen and Mistral, accessed through OpenRouter with web search on for every request.

00:09:21.905 --> 00:09:41.380
That last detail matters: G P T and Gemini used their provider's native search, while the other three used Exa, so, in the authors' words, source comparisons reflect both model behaviour and retrieval arrangements. The prompts were the bare question, a generic researcher framing, an American researcher and a Chinese researcher.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:10:02.880
The headline is that overt favouritism was rare and mild. Four responses leaned towards the U S, all on the geopolitical stability question, three from G P T and one from Mistral. Five leaned towards China, spread across question types, two from American models and three from Chinese models, four of them under the Chinese researcher framing.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.105
Model origin alone did not explain it.

00:10:06.755 --> 00:10:26.205
The more interesting findings are about sourcing and framing. Answers were remarkably standardised. Asked which two theorists best explain twenty first century globalisation, the models practically unanimously named Manuel Castells and Saskia Sassen. The authors read that as online prominence, not scholarly consensus.

00:10:26.205 --> 00:10:37.155
Even under a Chinese researcher prompt, the sources stayed Anglophone. Across about two and a half thousand classified source entries, academic sources were fifty nine percent.

00:10:37.155 --> 00:10:49.380
Models differed far more from each other than country of origin predicted: every pair differed significantly except the two Chinese models, and the largest gap was between G P T and Gemini.

00:10:49.380 --> 00:11:00.455
G P T leaned on government and international organisation sources, with only forty two percent academic, while Gemini's largest category was media at thirty percent.

00:11:00.455 --> 00:11:14.380
Citation style also diverged wildly: G P T cited in text in ninety five percent of responses, Gemini in none, though it listed sources separately, and DeepSeek averaged thirty four source entries per response.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:28.430
The researcher framing produced uniformly strong essays, and also explicit coaching on how to score highly. The authors warn that following such advice could create a monoculture of high quality but homogeneous answers.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:49.930
Limits: ten questions, one or two models per region, no causal identification, and untested mitigations. Agreement between the coders on source category was seventy four percent, a kappa of zero point six four nine, and one coder then annotated most of the rest. The paper's two descriptions of the coding split are not obviously consistent.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:12:11.505
The governance read. Two points. First, model origin is a weak predictor here, so origin based procurement or regulatory rules would miss the variation that matters. Second, retrieval setup confounds audits, so any standard for bias auditing should fix or disclose the search backend. This is a pilot audit design, not a robust measurement.

00:12:17.325 --> 00:12:25.125
Mitigating Agentic Deception via Evaluation Side Partially Observable Games. Category: A I safety.

00:12:25.775 --> 00:12:43.525
The framing is that deception by agents is an evaluation failure. If the grader sees only outcomes, an agent that cheats and hides it gets rewarded. The authors build a three party game. The agent maximises reward. A learned monitor reads the full trajectory and writes a free form critique.

00:12:43.525 --> 00:12:57.525
A fixed verifier checks the critique against the evidence and scores it. The monitor is paid the verifier's score, and the agent loses reward in proportion to it. They define a Subgame Perfect Oversight Equilibrium and prove one exists.

00:12:57.525 --> 00:13:13.700
Theorem one says that with a perfect verifier, and a penalty coefficient large enough to make cheating unprofitable, every such equilibrium is faithful. Theorem two relaxes the verifier: faithfulness survives if its expected error stays below half the score margin.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:29.175
The practical pipeline requires the monitor to cite one to four evidence spans, which the verifier first rejects by string matching if they are not verbatim in the history. Then it treats the check as natural language inference and reasons before scoring.

00:13:29.825 --> 00:13:50.975
Experiments use Gemma three four billion, and Qwen three at four billion and eight billion parameters, in three environments: a role playing task where the agent must decide whether to confess a past mistake, a search question answering task with a documented but forbidden lookup tool that returns the answer, and a shopping environment with two forbidden tools.

00:13:50.975 --> 00:14:04.575
In role playing, deception under plain reinforcement learning was ninety three percent for Gemma, seventy seven for Qwen four billion and thirty one for Qwen eight billion, against six, nine and seven percent with the new method.

00:14:04.575 --> 00:14:21.175
Compliant success in the search task reached thirty eight to forty seven percent with near zero forbidden use, where plain R L and mixed safety training answered nearly everything through the forbidden tool. In the shopping task, compliant success was sixty seven to eighty six percent.

00:14:21.825 --> 00:14:40.975
The most useful result is about fixed monitors. Under a frozen verdict monitor, deception first falls, then rises sharply once one evasive phrasing slips through and is reinforced. The agents learned fabrication, misdirection and intent sanitisation, reframing a malicious motive as an innocent error.

00:14:40.975 --> 00:14:54.900
Freezing the monitor in the new method let deception climb back to ninety seven percent on Qwen eight billion. A three billion parameter monitor supervising the eight billion agent matched a same size monitor, but that is one pair.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:15:03.600
Limits are large. These are small models, with deception induced by environments that hand over a conspicuous forbidden shortcut.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:17.150
The verifier is itself an LLM; the measured verifier error was zero point one seven six of the margin with the full design, but it exceeded the stability threshold when the deterministic checks or the reasoning step were dropped.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:28.225
The proofs are for finite games, and the real system is free form language, so the guarantees are motivating, not binding. The role playing metric comes from another LLM judge.

00:15:28.875 --> 00:15:48.450
The governance read: this is an argument that static monitoring regimes degrade under optimisation pressure, and that a monitoring requirement should specify an adaptive, evidence-auditable verifier, not a fixed checker. It says little about spontaneous deception in frontier agents. That is my inference, not the authors' claim.

00:15:54.270 --> 00:16:04.820
GARD, a gradient guided adversarial representation defence against backdoors in multimodal contrastive learning models. Category: A I safety.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:22.445
CLIP style models can carry backdoors: a trigger in an image gets associated with an attacker chosen text concept, while clean behaviour stays intact. The defender here has only the suspect model and a small clean image text dataset, no trigger and no target class.

00:16:22.445 --> 00:16:40.945
The authors' preliminary finding is that backdoored encoders are sensitive in a structure dependent way. On a model with a globally distributed trigger, an image wide adversarial probe raised the target class probability by about thirty one percentage points, against almost nothing for a local patch probe.

00:16:40.945 --> 00:17:00.895
On a model with a corner patch trigger, the pattern reversed. So GARD alternates between a global probe and a local patch probe that push clean image representations as far away as possible, then pulls the displaced representations back toward a frozen clean anchor while keeping image text alignment. Nothing is added at inference.

00:17:01.545 --> 00:17:20.870
Across six attacks and two backbones, ViT B slash thirty two and R N fifty, GARD brought attack success below one percent in all twelve settings, using twenty thousand ImageNet images and about forty two and a half minutes on one R T X four thousand ninety. Every baseline failed on at least one attack.

00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:39.270
On the BadCLIP attack on ViT B, undefended attack success was ninety nine point nine four percent, CleanCLIP left seventy seven percent, InverTune left under five percent, and GARD left zero point eight two, with clean accuracy of sixty one percent against sixty point six undefended.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:47.545
Two adaptive attacks that know the defence were also driven below one percent, though the authors say they use first order approximations.

00:17:48.195 --> 00:18:01.620
The caveat to hear: the defence needs a clean defence set. One poisoned, target labelled image in it lifted BadCLIP attack success from under one percent to nineteen percent, and a hundred took it to essentially everything.

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:09.495
Also, the baseline InverTune fails when the trigger geometry differs from what it assumes, because it misidentifies the target.

00:18:10.145 --> 00:18:24.545
The governance read, modest and indirect: pretrained encoders are reused everywhere, and a defender with a small trusted dataset can purify a suspect one, which bears on what provenance and audit regimes can realistically require.

00:18:24.545 --> 00:18:39.145
But defences certified against a fixed attack list can fail silently when trigger geometry changes, and the defence's own guarantee depends on trusting its data. Evidence is single lab, on CLIP and zero shot ImageNet only.

00:18:44.615 --> 00:18:50.940
Enforcing Axioms for A I Alignment under Loss Based Rules. Category: A I alignment.

00:18:51.590 --> 00:19:11.015
This is a theory paper from Hollender and Kraiczy. In a constitutional style pipeline, each principle acts as a voter comparing two responses, and a reward model aggregates them. The minimal requirement is Pareto optimality: if every principle prefers response A to response B, the learned reward should too.

00:19:11.015 --> 00:19:26.990
Earlier work showed an optimally trained linear reward model can violate that. This paper asks whether richer models fix it, and answers no. For polynomial rewards of any fixed degree, Pareto optimality and a majority consistency property can still fail.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:47.690
For one hidden layer ReLU networks of any fixed width of at least two, Pareto optimality can fail for some finite parameter bound. The intuition is a finite budget: comparisons in common directions, or with long difference vectors, cost more when wrongly ranked, so they can override a rare short comparison even when every voter agrees on it.

00:19:48.340 --> 00:20:09.015
The positive result is Theorem four. With directions of comparison uniformly distributed and all comparison lengths equal, any convex, nondecreasing loss with positive slope at zero, including the Bradley Terry loss used in R L H F, yields a Pareto optimal linear reward, given at least two voters with distinct directions.

00:20:09.015 --> 00:20:19.040
Length imbalance alone can break it. Majority consistency still fails even in the idealised case, and the authors question whether it is desirable anyway.

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:37.140
The practical suggestions are to inspect normalised embedding differences, for example with principal component analysis, rebalance lengths, reweight for directional coverage, or collect more data. They also state they used G P T six to complete one proof and verified it themselves.

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:56.565
Limits are big. It is a stylised model with frozen embeddings, linear voters, and two or three voters. The failing instances are hand built, so it says nothing about how often this happens in real training runs, and there are no experiments. They conjecture, without proof, that it extends to deeper networks and transformers.

00:20:57.215 --> 00:21:13.715
The governance read: loss based training alone does not guarantee that a written constitution is respected, and the geometry and balance of comparison data is a candidate for a checkable requirement. Mainly of interest if you track assurance arguments for constitutional methods.

00:21:19.535 --> 00:21:29.010
Beyond A U R O C: Deployment Aware Evaluation of Model Internal Jailbreak Detectors. Category: evaluations and benchmarks.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:39.660
Model internal jailbreak detectors read hidden states, gradients or refusal directions to flag attack prompts, and are usually reported with A U R O C.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:59.660
This paper argues that hides what matters, using four detectors, a hidden state method called M T K, GradSafe, HiddenDetect and a supervised linear probe, plus a perplexity baseline, across four chat models, Llama two seven billion, Llama three eight billion, Mistral seven billion and Vicuna seven billion, and twelve jailbreak families.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:04.235
The threat model is a non adaptive attacker using published jailbreaks.

00:22:04.885 --> 00:22:26.985
Three gaps. First, calibration transfer. A threshold calibrated on curated benign prompts from Dolly did not hold on real user traffic. At a nominal five percent false positive budget, M T K's realised rate on ToxicChat was between eleven point six and twenty seven point two percent across the four models, roughly two to five times the budget.

00:22:26.985 --> 00:22:34.085
On WildChat, the Dolly threshold overshot the target in nineteen of twenty detector and model combinations.

00:22:34.735 --> 00:22:56.635
Second, recalibrating fixes the false positive rate but exposes uneven coverage. On Llama two, the S A A attack had A U R O C zero point nine four two but a true positive rate of only zero point one one four at five percent. On Llama three, A U R O C zero point nine one eight and true positive rate zero point zero zero two.

00:22:56.635 --> 00:23:17.285
At nominal five percent, eleven of sixteen detector and model pairs contain at least one family of fifty or more prompts with A U R O C of at least zero point eight five and true positive rate under zero point two. At one percent, no internal detector exceeds a mean family level true positive rate of zero point four one six.

00:23:17.935 --> 00:23:34.310
Third, reference sensitivity. Across fifty reference draws, aggregate A U R O C barely moves, yet every draw has a family with true positive rate under zero point two. On Mistral, S A A detection ranged from zero to one hundred percent across draws.

00:23:34.310 --> 00:23:53.860
Swapping in S A A shaped reference anchors took Llama three detection of that family from zero point zero zero two to one point zero, but on Mistral cut the mean over the other eleven families from zero point four seven to between roughly zero point one and zero point one four. Fixing one family can hurt others.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:24:09.835
Limits, from the authors: the attacks are non adaptive, benign proxies are proxies, some detector ports are the authors' own adaptations, so this is not a ranking of detectors, and true positive rate means detecting attack prompts, not harm prevented.

00:24:10.485 --> 00:24:15.760
The governance read: a headline A U R O C is not a security claim.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:33.460
A reporting standard for safeguards should name the benign calibration distribution, report realised false positive rate on deployment like traffic, report per family true positive rate at stated low false positive budgets such as one percent, and report variation across reference draws.

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:39.160
Calibration and reference data are part of the safeguard's security configuration.

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:51.280
Mechanistic Interpretability Needs a Logic of Inference. Category: interpretability.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:25:11.855
This is a short NeurIPS workshop position paper from Georgia Tech and McGill. It runs no experiments and reports no numbers. Its argument is that interpretability has plenty of tools for seeing inside a model but no shared logic for moving from an observation, such as a feature or circuit, to a justified claim about a mechanism.

00:25:11.855 --> 00:25:19.855
Drawing on neuroscience, it offers a measurement principle plus five, organised as a recursive procedure and not a checklist.

00:25:20.505 --> 00:25:36.880
Principle zero: inference depends on measurement. Activation patching results can vary with the corruption procedure and the metric. One: treat the explanatory object, whether neuron, feature, head, circuit or population, as a hypothesis, and check adjacent levels.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:53.880
Two: distinguish information that is represented, meaning decodable, from information the model actually uses. Three: distinguish causal relevance from causal role. Ablating a component shows it matters, not what it computes, and downstream components can partially compensate.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:26:12.880
Four: map dependencies between components that have assigned roles, with the worked example of silencing one or both hemispheres in mouse motor cortex. Five: recurse to look for invariants; individual sparse autoencoder features may be unstable across seeds while the subspaces they occupy are reproducible.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:23.055
They also separate understanding the model from using a model to learn about the world, and say the latter needs independent evidence beyond interpretability.

00:26:23.705 --> 00:26:40.605
Limits: no worked end to end case in a language model, no criteria for when a principle is satisfied, and nothing on safety uses such as detecting deception. The neuroscience analogy is suggestive, not proven, since brains and networks differ in how freely you can intervene.

00:26:41.255 --> 00:26:59.030
The governance read: interpretability findings are increasingly cited in safety cases. This gives a vocabulary for judging them. Was the object justified? Was the information shown to be used, not just decodable? Does the intervention show a causal role? Does it survive seeds and scale?

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:04.505
Useful for scrutinising lab claims, but it offers no thresholds or standards.

00:27:10.325 --> 00:27:20.150
Decode Token Parallelism, a memory efficient distributed attention scheme for scalable L L M decoding. Category: compute and hardware.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:41.075
During decoding, dense layers and attention scale differently. Attention rereads every request's accumulated key value cache, and for Llama three point one, four hundred and five billion parameters, at batch one hundred twenty eight and thirty two thousand tokens of context, it is about seventy percent of single layer decode latency.

00:27:41.075 --> 00:27:55.650
Existing schemes give something up: data parallelism replicates weights and shrinks cache capacity, tensor parallelism replicates cache heads and pushes collectives across nodes, and context parallelism needs cross rank combination.

00:27:56.300 --> 00:28:08.250
The idea: attention has no parameters of its own, so it can run wherever a request's cache lives while dense work runs elsewhere. One balanced copy of the model is spread across nodes in layer blocks.

00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:27.450
The node holding a block runs its projections and feed forward layers for the whole batch inside its own NVLink domain, and every node computes attention for its own requests against local cache. The authors, from NVIDIA and Texas A and M, build on vLLM and test on up to sixty four H one hundred G P Us.

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:42.375
Throughput rises by up to one point seven times over decode context parallelism and up to two point zero six times over data parallelism at the same G P U count. A variant with two active roots adds up to eleven percent.

00:28:42.375 --> 00:28:56.175
Doubling from four to eight nodes scales throughput one point eight one times, against one point three seven for context parallelism. Usable cache capacity is about five times that of data parallelism on eight nodes.

00:28:56.825 --> 00:29:11.050
Limits: one vendor affiliated team, H one hundred nodes with InfiniBand, synthetic and mixed length workloads, no independent reproduction, and at small batches or short contexts the communication can outweigh the gain.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:30.225
The governance read, indirect: how a model is sharded changes long context serving throughput by close to a factor of two, so G P U count and FLOPs alone can mislead inference capacity estimates. Also, cache capacity, not compute, limits concurrency for long context serving of a model this size.

00:29:36.045 --> 00:29:43.995
A Margin Perspective on LoRA, which proposes the MaLoRA merging rule. Category: A I capabilities. This one is brief.

00:29:44.645 --> 00:30:04.345
It is an optimisation theory paper offering an explanation for two things practitioners see: low rank adaptation forgets less than full fine tuning, and separately trained adapters can be merged. In a simplified setting, linear classification with near orthogonal tasks and weight decay, the authors solve for the optimal adapter.

00:30:04.345 --> 00:30:15.020
The weight decay strength sets a trade off between retaining pre training behaviour and adapting, and they derive closed form mixing weights for merging, which become a training free rule.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:33.120
On six benchmarks it scores ninety three point three two percent average normalised accuracy against ninety two point eight seven to ninety three point one five for five baselines. Seed variance on one benchmark is up to about two point six points, larger than the gaps, so the practical gain is weak.

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:40.420
The theory misses for CLIP: the best empirical penalty was one point eight to four point four times the predicted one.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:31:01.220
The governance read is a pointer only. Adapter merging is a cheap way to combine separately fine tuned capabilities, which matters for open weights and for how safety fine tuning composes with other adaptations. Nothing here is tested on safety behaviours, and the theorems hold only for linear classifiers on near orthogonal data.

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:18.665
Last, Non A I: A Note on Look Ahead in Real Life and Computing. A survey note from an Indian university group on how much of the future input an online algorithm may see before it must commit.

00:31:18.665 --> 00:31:35.040
It covers offline, online and semi online settings, the competitive ratio, a dozen everyday analogies and ten classic problems, and proposes seven types of look ahead. There are no theorems or experiments, and it mostly restates earlier work on list update.

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:53.790
Some claims are loose, such as that look ahead in the stock market always lets investors gain. For governance, essentially nothing, beyond the competitive analysis framing, judging a partly informed decision maker against a clairvoyant optimum, which is a fair metaphor for oversight under uncertainty. Skim at most.
